 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**No, not yet.** Inflation has picked back up over the past year and is running above the RBA's 2–3% target band.\n\n- **Headline CPI:** 4.0% year-on-year in August 2026 (latest monthly figure). It peaked at 4.6% in March 2026 and dipped to 3.5% in July before rising again.\n- **Trimmed mean (the RBA's preferred underlying measure):** 3.6% in August 2026. That is up from a low of 2.8% in June 2025 and has risen for most of the past year.\n- **Policy response:** The cash rate target was 3.60% from around September 2025 to January 2026. It has since risen to 4.36% (September 2026 monthly average), an increase of about 0.75 percentage points.\n\nInflation was clearly coming under control in mid-2025, when the trimmed mean fell to 2.8%. That progress has reversed. The underlying measure has been above 3% since late 2025 and is still rising, so the RBA is tightening to bring it back down.",
